[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for getting a `.pmtiles` archive onto a MapLibre GL JS map.  
It works for vector, raster, and raster-DEM archives and is compatible with the current v4+ `addProtocol` API.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler (`new pmtiles.Protocol()`) that knows how to do the HTTP **Range** requests required by the format.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the `pmtiles://` protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it with MapLibre
// The handler (`pmtilesProtocol.tile`) already contains the v3-v6 compatibility shim
// so it works with the promise-based signature required by MapLibre ≥ 4.0.
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why only once?**  
> `addProtocol` registers a global handler. Adding it inside a component’s render loop will create duplicate handlers and can leak memory.

If you ever need to clean up (e.g. on a single-page-app teardown):

```js
maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

---

## 3️⃣ Prepare the source definition in your style

### a) Vector tiles

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "myVector": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "water",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "myVector",
      "source-layer": "water",          // <-- name of the layer inside the archive
      "paint": { "fill-color": "#a0c8f0" }
    },
    {
      "id": "roads",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "myVector",
      "source-layer": "road",
      "paint": { "line-color": "#777", "line-width": 1 }
    }
    // …more layers that reference the same source
  ]
}
```

### b) Raster imagery

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "satellite": {
      "type": "raster",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery/usa.pmtiles",
      "tileSize": 256
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "sat",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "satellite"
    }
  ]
}
```

### c) Raster-DEM (terrain)

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "elevation": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elev/europe.pmtiles",
      "encoding": "terrarium"   // or "mapbox"
    }
  },
  "terrain": {
    "source": "elevation",
    "exaggeration": 1.5
  }
}
```

**Important:** Use **`url:`** (not `tiles:`).  
`pmtiles://…` tells the protocol to read the archive’s header, which supplies the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`. If you use a `tiles:` template you lose that information and MapLibre will assume a default max-zoom of 22, causing unnecessary empty-tile requests.

---

## 4️⃣ Create the map

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// The protocol registration from step 2 must run before this
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the <div>
  style: 'style.json',       // the JSON from step 3 (or an inline object)
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

If you prefer an **inline style** instead of a separate `style.json` file:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: { /* copy the source object from step 3 */ },
    layers:  [ /* copy the layers array from step 3 */ ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

---

## 5️⃣ Hosting requirements for the `.pmtiles` file

| Requirement | Why it matters |
|-------------|----------------|
| **HTTP Range support** (`Accept-Ranges: bytes`) | The client fetches only the byte range that contains the requested tile. |
| **CORS headers** (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain) | Browsers enforce cross-origin checks for the range request. |
| **Cache-Control** (e.g. `public, max-age=31536000`) | The file is immutable; long-term caching speeds up subsequent loads. |
| **Static host** (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, any CDN) | No server-side logic needed; the file can be served directly. |

> **Tip:** Put the file behind a CDN (CloudFront, Cloudflare) so the range responses are cached at edge locations.

---

## 6️⃣ Debugging checklist

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Tiles stay gray / no data | Protocol not registered or registered after map creation | Ensure `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', …)` runs **before** `new maplibregl.Map()`. |
| 404 on tile request | Wrong URL or the host does not support Range requests | Verify the URL works in a browser and that the response includes `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. |
| CORS error in console | Host missing `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header | Add the header (most static hosts let you set it via bucket/hosting settings). |
| Over-zoom shows blank tiles | Used `tiles:` instead of `url:` or the archive’s `maxzoom` is lower than the map’s zoom | Switch to `url:`; MapLibre will automatically over-zoom using the highest available zoom in the archive. |
| “Tile fetch error: …” from the protocol handler | AbortController cancelled (e.g., rapid pan/zoom) or network failure | This is normal; the handler will retry when the map asks for the tile again. |
| `data` property missing in handler result | Custom handler returning a raw `ArrayBuffer` (pre-v4 style) | Return `{ data: buffer }` (or use `pmtilesProtocol.tile` which already does this). |

---

## 7️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Create map with a vector PMTiles source
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'land',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'land',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#eae5d5' }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `https://example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles` with the URL of **your** archive.

