## 8️⃣ Quick “cheat sheet” of the essential commands

| What you need | Code / CLI |
|---------------|------------|
| Install PMTiles library | `npm i pmtiles` |
| Register protocol (v4+) | `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);` |
| Vector source definition | `"type":"vector", "url":"pmtiles://https://…/my.pmtiles"` |
| Raster source definition | `"type":"raster", "url":"pmtiles://https://…/my.pmtiles"` |
| Raster-DEM source definition | `"type":"raster-dem", "url":"pmtiles://https://…/my.pmtiles", "encoding":"terrarium"` |
| Verify a PMTiles file locally | `pmtiles verify my.pmtiles` |
| Inspect layer names | `pmtiles show my.pmtiles --metadata` (look at `vector_layers[].id`) |
| Extract a region (optional) | `pmtiles extract my.pmtiles out.pmtiles --bbox=-10,35,10,45 --minzoom=5 --maxzoom=12` |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Install** `pmtiles`.
2. **Add** the protocol: `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);`
3. **Reference** the archive with `url: "pmtiles://https://…/my.pmtiles"` in a `vector`, `raster`, or `raster-dem` source.
4. **Host** the file on any static server that supports HTTP range requests and CORS.
5. **Create** the map – the protocol handler will fetch only the needed tile bytes, and MapLibre will render them just like any other tile source.

That’s all you need to load a `.pmtiles` file into MapLibre GL JS! 🚀

## What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

`maplibregl.addProtocol()` used to accept a **callback-style** handler:

```js
// v3
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

Starting with **v4** the API became **promise-based** and the handler must return an **object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes).  
The second argument is now an **`AbortController`** (instead of a callback) so the handler can be cancelled when the map aborts a request.

If you keep the old callback signature the request resolves to an empty tile – MapLibre sees no `data` field, treats the response as “no tile”, and you get blank tiles (or a silent error).

---

## Quick fix – rewrite the handler to the new contract

```js
// -------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Register the protocol (once, before any map)
// -------------------------------------------------
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  // params.url is the full URL that the style asked for,
  // e.g. "https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
  // abortController.signal can be passed to fetch so the request
  // can be cancelled by the map.

  const response = await fetch(params.url, {
    signal: abortController.signal,
    // If you need custom headers, add them here:
    // headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${myToken}` }
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the request and MapLibre will treat it as a tile error.
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // Get the raw bytes (ArrayBuffer, Uint8Array, Blob, or Response are all OK)
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // The contract: return an object with a `data` property.
  // Optional: cacheControl / expires can be added if you want to control HTTP caching.
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

### What the return object can contain

| Property | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `data` (required) | `ArrayBuffer`, `Uint8Array`, `Blob`, or a `Response` object that contains the tile bytes. |
| `cacheControl` (optional) | String that will be used as the `Cache-Control` header for this tile. |
| `expires` (optional) | Number (epoch ms) or `Date` that will be used as the `Expires` header. |

If you only need the raw bytes, `{ data: buffer }` is enough.

---

## Full example – a custom “XYZ” source that adds an API key

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Register once (e.g. in your app’s entry point)
const apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY';

maplibregl.addProtocol('xyz', async (params, abortController) => {
  // The style will request something like:
  //   xyz://https://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
  // `params.url` already contains the interpolated {z}/{x}/{y}
  const url = new URL(params.url);
  url.searchParams.set('key', apiKey);   // add your auth token

  const response = await fetch(url.toString(), {
    signal: abortController.signal,
    // you can also set mode:'cors', credentials:'include', etc.
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Tile error ${response.status}`);
  }

  const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();
  return { data: arrayBuffer };
});
```

**Style snippet that uses the protocol**

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "myTiles": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "xyz://https://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "roads",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "myTiles",
      "source-layer": "road",
      "paint": { "line-color": "#777", "line-width": 2 }
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## If you already use the **pmtiles** library – you don’t need to rewrite anything

The `pmtiles` npm package ships a **v3-compat shim** that detects whether the second argument is a callback or an `AbortController` and adapts automatically. The one-liner that works on **v3, v4, v5 and v6** is:

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();   // creates the handler
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile); // <-- works on all versions
```

If you were using an older `pmtiles` (< 3.0.0) you must **upgrade** to at least `pmtiles@3` so the shim is present. After that you can keep the same style (`pmtiles://https://example.com/data.pmtiles`) and the library will handle the new promise-based contract for you.

---

## Step-by-step migration checklist

| ✅ What to do | 📌 Why |
|--------------|--------|
| **1. Upgrade the `pmtiles` package** (`npm i pmtiles@latest`) | The v3+ package includes the `v3compat` shim that works with MapLibre v4+. |
| **2. If you wrote your own handler, replace the callback** with an `async (params, abortController) => { … return {data}` | MapLibre v4 expects a promise that resolves to `{data}`. |
| **3. Pass `abortController.signal` to `fetch` (or your XHR library)** | Allows the map to cancel in-flight requests when the user pans/zooms quickly. |
| **4. Return `{data: …}`** (ArrayBuffer/Uint8Array/Blob/Response) | The map reads the `data` field; a bare buffer is ignored. |
| **5. Register the protocol **once** before any `new maplibregl.Map()`** | The protocol registry is global; registering multiple times is harmless but unnecessary. |
| **6. Test** – open the console, look for `TileError` messages. If you still see “Tile request failed” check that the URL is correct and that CORS/Range headers are present on the server. | Guarantees the new handler is being called. |
| **7. (Optional) Add `cacheControl` / `expires`** if you want fine-grained HTTP caching per tile. | Improves CDN behavior. |

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Blank vector tiles** (no features, no error) | Handler returned a raw `ArrayBuffer` instead of `{data: buffer}`. | Wrap the buffer in `{data: buffer}`. |
| **`TileError: request aborted`** even though the network shows a 200 | The handler ignored the `AbortController` and kept the fetch alive, then MapLibre aborted it. | Pass `signal: abortController.signal` to `fetch`. |
| **CORS errors** (`No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header`) | The host of the `.pmtiles` file (or the tile server you proxy to) does not send CORS headers. | Configure the static host (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, etc.) to send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. |
| **Range requests not honored** (tiles always 404) | The server does not support HTTP `Range` (or strips the header). | Use a host that supports byte-range requests (S3, R2, most CDNs). |
| **Using `tiles:` instead of `url:` in the source** | `tiles:` bypasses the PMTiles header that contains min/max zoom, causing the map to request zoom levels that don’t exist. | Always use `url:` for PMTiles sources (`url: "pmtiles://https://…/my.pmtiles"`). |
| **Old `pmtiles` version (2.x) still uses callbacks** | The library’s `protocol.tile` still expects a callback, which MapLibre v4 will not call. | Upgrade to `pmtiles@≥3`. |

---

## Full minimal reproducible example (React-ish but works in plain JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles (v4)</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol (once)
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile); // works on v4+

  // 2️⃣ Create the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        basemap: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://YOUR_BUCKET.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'basemap',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'basemap',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `https://YOUR_BUCKET.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles` with the URL of a PMTiles file that is publicly readable and serves `Range` requests.*

---

## TL;DR – the one-liner you need

```js
// If you only use PMTiles:
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();   // v3+ includes the compatibility shim
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile); // works on MapLibre v4+
```

If you have a **custom** source, replace the old callback with an `async` function that returns `{data: …}` and forwards `abortController.signal` to `fetch`.

That’s all you need to get your custom tiles (or PMTiles) working again after the v3→v4 upgrade. Happy mapping!
